{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-217279","model_call":8,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ military soldiers servicemembers) (+ \"paid so little\" \"paid poorly\" underpaid) (+ \"why\" reasons))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ military soldiers servicemembers) (+ \"paid so little\" \"paid poorly\" underpaid) (+ \"why\" reasons))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_2407476165#16_3193233423","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1639","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_2407476165#16_3193233423\" , \"end_char\": 9668.000000 , \"headings\": \"Why were Germans called \\\"Jerry\\\" in WWI? Why were Germans called \\\"Jerry\\\" in WWI? Footnotes Main reason: \" , \"segment\": \"Many British <cover>soldiers thought the Stahlhelm resembled a chamber pot, which was itself known by the euthemism: Jeroboam. 1.3K views · Answer requested by Sasha Trisinky Jeffery Bailey , former Unit Historian (1991-2008) Answered 3 years ago · Author has 203 answers and 639.9K answer views Originally Answered: In ww1 why were the Germans called Jerry? The Germans actually were very seldom called Jerry in WWI. The tea slurping, biscuit biting, pinky pointing, undersexed, underpaid </cover>and underwhelming individuals from England almost universally called the Germans the “Huns”, in WWI, The only time they didn’t call Germans “Huns” were when they were calling Austrians “Huns”. Bright and inventive, huh? Helps you to understand how these bright boys from Britain managed to lose an ocean liner by running it into an iceberg, on a clear, April night. 3.2K views · View upvotes · Answer requested by Bryan "},"time":4425},"model_calls":8,"document_judgments":6,"relevance_3":0,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
