{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-217279","model_call":36,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"military pay\" \"basic pay\") (+ low \"lowest rung\" \"training status\") (+ meals housing provided))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"military pay\" \"basic pay\") (+ low \"lowest rung\" \"training status\") (+ meals housing provided))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_1554232894#0_2866862651","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1657","rank":1,"snippet":": \"Military Pay Military Pay MILITARY PAY CHART: ENLISTED Scaling Up Your Military Paychecks RELATED ARTICLES: \" , \"segment\": \"Military Pay: Enlisted Military Salary Military Pay MILITARY PAY CHART: ENLISTED Scaling Up Your Military Paychecks Pay Grade: E-1 to E-5 | E-6 to E-9 The enlisted pay chart starts with basic pay rates for all those who have been in the service for four months or less. That's the amount of time most people spend in basic training, and it's the very <cover>lowest rung of the military pay scale. There's a good reason for that--all meals </cover>and housing are provided, so you don't get any military allowances. Pay at the lowest rank for those in training status is considered low compared to those on active duty; just under $1300 per month according to the 2009 enlisted military pay chart. Once you've served more than four months, your pay goes up depending on what rank you were given when you enter basic training. Most enter military service with a rank of E1;\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_1554232894#0_2866862651","rank":1,"relevance":2}]},"time":1068},"model_calls":36,"document_judgments":30,"relevance_3":12,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-217279","model_call":36,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1657"},"model_calls":36,"document_judgments":30,"relevance_3":12,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-217279","model_call":36,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1657"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1657"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_678495283#2_1403068542","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1657","rank":2,"snippet":"Prequalify today! About Military Pay Two main factors affect how military pay is calculated: years of service and rank, which generally corresponds with military pay grade. Basic pay rates are calculated monthly, rather than weekly or bimonthly. Monthly pay is automatically split in half and distributed twice a month, some branches allow service members to opt to receive a monthly lump sum instead. Take-home pay is impacted by automatic deductions for things like taxes, garnishments, Savings Deposit Program (SDP) and Thrift Savings Plan contributions. Basic Compensation Package The basic compensation package provided to all service members includes: <cover>Basic pay Housing allowance (or government-provided housing) Subsistence allowance (or government-provided meals) Free medical and dental care for service members Free or low-</cover>cost medical and dental care for dependents Paid annual leave Allowances Allowances are the second most important part of military pay after Basic Pay and most allowances are non-taxable. Allowances provide for specific needs, such as food or housing.\" , \"start_char\": 1104.000000 , \"title\": \"Military Pay Calculator | Military Benefits\" , \"url\": \"https://militarybenefits.info/military-pay-calculator/\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_1554232894#0_2866862651","rank":1,"relevance":2}]},"time":9},"model_calls":36,"document_judgments":30,"relevance_3":12,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
